    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on some deck plans for a client in vancouver. it's a cedar deck attached to a house. i need to make sure my math is right for the load specs before i submit for permits. can you explain the general difference between how i should calculate live load vs dead load for the beam and post spacing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dead load accounts for the static weight of your materials, such as the cedar, decking, and structural components themselves. You calculate this by multiplying the volume and density of your materials. Live load, conversely, accounts for temporary, movable weight like people, furniture, or snow loads specific to the Vancouver area. You must use the local building code's prescribed live load values for Vancouver when determining the required support spacing.",
      "source": "target",
